  <!-- ── LA FAILURE PATTERNS ── -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow-sub">LA conditions</p>
      <h2>Why Pitco units fail in patterns specific to LA commercial operations</h2>
      <p>Pitco publishes service-life expectations based on national-average operating conditions. LA conditions vary the failure timing across three failure vectors.</p>

      <p><strong>High-volume duty cycles in stadium concessions and LAX flight catering.</strong> Stadium concession Megafry and Multi-Battery configurations push 16-plus hour daily cooking cycles during operating season. LAX flight catering operates essentially 24/7. National service-life estimates assume 6 to 10 hours per day. The duty-cycle math accelerates wear roughly 1.5 to 2x on FilterMate pumps, gas valves, and oil temperature probes for high-volume installations.</p>

      <p><strong>Ethnic-cuisine sediment-heavy frying.</strong> K-Town fried chicken operations, Asian seafood restaurants, Latin fish-and-chips concepts push sediment-heavy fry work through Pitco SFSG and SG configurations. Sediment accumulation accelerates oil-return-valve fouling, FilterMate paper degradation, and burner port carbon buildup. We see SFSG units in heavy ethnic-cuisine duty needing FilterMate or external filtration service at twice the rate of standard mid-tier restaurant configurations.</p>

      <p><strong>Coastal salt-air corrosion.</strong> Properties within roughly a mile of the Pacific — Santa Monica, Venice, Marina del Rey, Manhattan Beach, Newport Beach — see accelerated corrosion on gas regulator components and exposed metallic hardware. Bi-annual inspection is the recommended schedule for coastal Pitco installations; we catch corrosion progression at the regulator and exposed components.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

