  <!-- ── FAQ ── -->
  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow-sub">FAQ</p>
      <h2>Pitco Frialator service questions LA operators ask</h2>

      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>Is Pitco part of Middleby, Welbilt, or Ali Group?</summary>
        <p>Middleby. Pitco Frialator is part of The Middleby Corporation family. Acquisition chain: Blodgett acquired Pitco 1981; Middleby acquired Blodgett early 2000s; Pitco has been Middleby for roughly two decades. Pitco was NEVER part of Welbilt or Ali Group — common SERP confusion. The Welbilt → Ali Group July 2022 transition moved Frymaster, Garland, Manitowoc, Lincoln, Convotherm, Kolpak, and others — Pitco was never in the Welbilt portfolio. Middleby siblings: Ultrafryer + Anets (fryers), Blodgett (ovens), Southbend (ranges), Imperial, Viking Commercial, TurboChef, MagiKitch'n, Wells.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What's the difference between Pitco Solstice Supreme and Frymaster FilterQuick?</summary>
        <p>Different mechanisms, different corporate families. Solstice Supreme uses FilterMate (pump-and-paper auto-filter with O-ring sealed manifold). FilterQuick (FQE/FQG) uses a manifold-based architecture. Pitco is Middleby family (via Blodgett 1981); Frymaster is Ali Group family (via Welbilt 2022) — cross-corporate competitors, not siblings. Different parts pipelines: Middleby distribution vs Ali Group NA Winston-Salem. For LA: McDonald's franchises spec Frymaster (non-negotiable); independents and non-McDonald's chains tend toward Pitco. We service both lines.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I tell if my Pitco SG14 needs a gas valve or a thermocouple?</summary>
        <p>Pilot behavior is the diagnostic key. Pilot lights and stays lit but main burner doesn't fire (or fires weakly): thermocouple is the most likely failure, $180-$320. Pilot won't light or drops within seconds: thermopile or thermocouple. Pilot lights, main burner fires, but oil temperature unstable: gas valve modulation, $300-$520. SG14 is the most-searched Pitco model by name. Our techs carry SG14-spec thermocouples, thermopiles, and gas valve assemblies on the truck for same-visit resolution.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What's the "Frialator" on my Pitco? Is it different from regular Pitco?</summary>
        <p>Same brand, two names. "Pitco Frialator" is the formal trade name; "Pitco" is the informal short form. Frialator comes from a 1959 invention — Pitco invented and patented the Frialator design in 1959 and the name became the brand identity. Social-media handles: @pitcofrialator on Facebook/Instagram, @pitco_frialator on X. Legal entity: Pitco Frialator, Inc. Parts catalogs use both interchangeably — a "Frialator SG14 thermocouple" and a "Pitco SG14 thermocouple" are the same OEM part.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can I still get parts for my 1990s Pitco 35C+ or VF Series legacy fryer?</summary>
        <p>Yes for most common wear parts. Pitco's parts availability through Middleby distribution remains unusually strong for older units. Common 35C+ and VF components (thermocouples, burner ignitors, gas valves, pilot assemblies, oil temperature probes, door gaskets) remain stocked. Specialty components sometimes ship from secondary sources with 5-10 day lead times. For 25+ year units, repair-vs-replace depends on what fails — thermocouple swap runs $200-$340 (worth doing); full sealed-system or control-board failure may push toward replacement. We diagnose and quote both options.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Pitco Solstice Supreme touch screen unresponsive — full display swap or sensor strip?</summary>
        <p>Diagnose the sensor strip first. The Solstice Supreme touch interface uses a capacitive sensor strip behind the front display glass — heat, steam, and cleaning chemistry can damage the strip while the underlying display + processor are fine. We test independently. Strip replacement runs $300-$500 (part + 45-60 min labor). Full display module replacement runs $700-$1,100. The "just replace the whole module for $1,400-$1,800" quote elsewhere is sometimes correct, but more often the sensor strip catches the actual failure at a fraction of the cost. Same pattern on TorQ and ROV current-catalog touch controls.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What does Pitco repair cost in LA, and is repair cheaper than replacement?</summary>
        <p>$120 flat commercial diagnostic, waived with repair. Typical ranges: thermocouple $180-$320, oil temp probe $200-$340, burner ignitor $220-$380, gas regulator $220-$380, FilterMate paper+O-ring $180-$320, sensor strip $300-$500, gas valve $300-$520, FilterMate pump motor $380-$640, full display module $700-$1,100, control board $700-$1,250. Most calls land $350-$800. New Solstice Supreme 14 runs $12,000-$18,000 installed; new SG14 runs $4,500-$6,500. Repair math almost always favors repair through year 12 to 15. Cascading failures (control board + FilterMate pump + thermocouples in quick succession) sometimes flip toward replacement. We give honest assessments — no upsell to repair when replacement makes more sense.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>My Pitco fryer won't heat or won't hold oil temperature. What's the first thing to check?</summary>
        <p>Start with the pilot. On a gas Pitco (SG14, Solstice), if the pilot lights but the main burner won't fire, the thermocouple is the most likely failure ($180-$320); if the pilot won't light or drops out, the thermopile. If the burner fires but oil temperature is unstable or never reaches setpoint, it's the gas-valve modulation circuit ($300-$520) or a drifting oil-temperature probe ($200-$340). On electric SE-series units, the heating element ($320-$520) or contactor ($180-$280). Our techs follow the pilot-behavior diagnostic and carry SG14 thermocouples, thermopiles, and gas valves on the truck. The $120 diagnostic is waived with repair.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Why is my Pitco FilterMate leaking or no longer filtering the oil?</summary>
        <p>On a Solstice Supreme the FilterMate pump-and-paper system is the usual source. Most common fix: filter paper plus the O-ring sealed manifold — out-of-spec paper or a worn O-ring lets oil bypass or leak, $180-$320. Next: oil return valve ($300-$480) and, on higher-hour units, the FilterMate pump motor (year 5-8, $380-$640); a wash-pump rebuild runs $450-$720. We check paper, O-ring, suction path, and pump amperage under load before quoting, and use OEM Pitco parts. The $120 diagnostic is waived with repair.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
